{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the ruler of the County of Tripoli in the 12th century. He was a member of the House of Hauteville and was a nephew of Roger II of Sicily. Raymond III was born in 1105 in Naples Italy and died in 1152 in Tripoli \n\n**Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III became the ruler of the County of Tripoli in 1149 after the death of his brother Ponce I. He was a capable military leader and expanded the county's borders through a series of military campaigns. In 1149 he led a successful campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate and captured several important cities including Mahdia in Tunisia and Tripoli in the year 1150 \n\n**Politics and Alliances**\n\nRaymond III was a skilled politician and formed alliances with other powerful rulers in the region. In 1149 he formed an alliance with the Holy Roman Emperor Conrad III and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. This alliance allowed him to secure the support of the Holy Roman Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem in his military campaigns. Raymond III also formed alliances with other rulers including the County of Edessa and the Kingdom of Sicily \n\n**Military Campaigns and Legacy**\n\nRaymond III led several successful military campaigns during his reign including a campaign against the Fatimid Caliphate in 1149 and a campaign against the Ayyubid dynasty in 1151. He also led a successful defense of the County of Tripoli against the attacks of the Ayyubid dynasty. Raymond III's military campaigns helped to secure the County of Tripoli and establish it as a major power in the region. He died in 1152 and was succeeded by his brother Bohemond II.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001284993631998077}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.19242680072784424}, {"token": " of", "logprob"